a>\n191\t<ol class=\"ltx_toclist ltx_toclist_section\">\n192\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S3.SS1\" title=\"In 3 VQ-VAE on Pitch Contour ‣ Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">3.1 </span>Receptive Field Separation</span></a></li>\n193\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S3.SS2\" title=\"In 3 VQ-VAE on Pitch Contour ‣ Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">3.2 </span>Transformation-Minimized Reconstruction Loss</span></a></li>\n194\t</ol></li>\n195\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_section\"><a href=\"#S4\" title=\"In Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">4 </span>Experimental Setup</span></a>\n196\t<ol class=\"ltx_toclist ltx_toclist_section\">\n197\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S4.SS1\" title=\"In 4 Experimental Setup ‣ Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">4.1 </span>Dataset</span></a>\n198\t<ol class=\"ltx_toclist ltx_toclist_subsection\">\n199\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsubsection\"><a href=\"#S4.SS1.SSS1\" title=\"In 4.1 Dataset ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">4.1.1 </span>Pre-processing</span></a></li>\n200\t</ol></li>\n201\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S4.SS2\" title=\"In 4 Experimental Setup ‣ Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">4.2 </span>Model Specification</span></a></li>\n202\t</ol></li>\n203\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_section\"><a href=\"#S5\" title=\"In Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">5 </span>Segmentation Shift Consistency</span></a>\n204\t<ol class=\"ltx_toclist ltx_toclist_section\">\n205\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S5.SS1\" title=\"In 5 Segmentation Shift Consistency ‣ Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">5.1 </span>Experimental Dataset Construction</span></a></li>\n206\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S5.SS2\" title=\"In 5 Segmentation Shift Consistency ‣ Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">5.2 </span>Evaluation Metrics</span></a></li>\n207\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S5.SS3\" title=\"In 5 Segmentation Shift Consistency ‣ Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">5.3 </span>Results</span></a></li>\n208\t</ol></li>\n209\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_section\"><a href=\"#S6\" title=\"In Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">6 </span>Sigimsae Classification</span></a>\n210\t<ol class=\"ltx_toclist ltx_toclist_section\">\n211\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S6.SS1\" title=\"In 6 Sigimsae Classification ‣ Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">6.1 </span>Sigimsae Labeled Dataset</span></a></li>\n212\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S6.SS2\" title=\"In 6 Sigimsae Classification ‣ Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">6.2 </span>Token-to-label lookup classifier</span></a></li>\n213\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection\"><a href=\"#S6.SS3\" title=\"In 6 Sigimsae Classification ‣ Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">6.3 </span>Results</span></a></li>\n214\t</ol></li>\n215\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_section\"><a href=\"#S7\" title=\"In Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">7 </span>Token Analysis in Pansori</span></a></li>\n216\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_section\"><a href=\"#S8\" title=\"In Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">8 </span>Conclusion and Future Work</span></a></li>\n217\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_section\"><a href=\"#S9\" title=\"In Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref\">9 </span>Acknowledgement</span></a></li>\n218\t<li class=\"ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_bibliography\"><a href=\"#bib\" title=\"In Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_title\">References</span></a></li>\n219\t</ol></nav>\n220\t</nav>\n221\t<div class=\"ltx_page_main\">\n222\t<div id=\"infobox\" class=\"infobox\">\n223\t  <a id=\"license-tr\" href=\"https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available\">\n224\t    License: CC BY 4.0\n225\t  </a>\n226\t  <div id=\"watermark-tr\">\n227\tarXiv:2608.10979v1 [cs.SD] 11 Aug 2026</div>\n228\t</div><div class=\"ltx_page_content\">\n229\t<article class=\"ltx_document ltx_authors_1line\">\n230\t<h1 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_document\">Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis</h1>\n231\t\n232\t<div id=\"abstract1\" class=\"ltx_abstract\"><h6 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_abstract\">Abstract</h6>\n233\t    \n234\t<p id=\"abstract1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Computational analysis of music often relies on discrete representations, yet many musical traditions are organized around continuous pitch movement that resists segmentation into note-like units. For such traditions, the discrete units that analysis would build on are not given in advance. We address this gap by learning a vocabulary of local pitch-contour patterns directly from unlabeled audio, using a VQ-VAE that quantizes fixed-length contour segments into a finite codebook. To make the learned tokens stable across segmentation positions and small variations in timing and pitch range, we train the model with a reconstruction objective evaluated under the best alignment among a set of candidate temporal and pitch-domain transformations. Applied to Korean traditional music, the learned tokens recover information about expert-defined <span id=\"abstract1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">sigimsae</span> categories without supervision, and in <span id=\"abstract1.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">pansori</span> individual tokens align with the two principal modes, <span id=\"abstract1.1.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Gyemyeonjo</span> and <span id=\"abstract1.1.4\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Ujo</span>, supporting their use as units for corpus-level analysis of contour-centric traditions.</p>\n235\t  \n236\t</div>\n237\t<section id=\"S1\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n238\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">1 </span>Introduction</h2>\n239\t\n240\t<figure id=\"S1.F1\" class=\"ltx_figure\"><img src=\"2608.10979v1/overview_re.png\" id=\"S1.F1.g1\" class=\"ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait\" width=\"608\" height=\"905\" alt=\"alt.\">\n241\t<figcaption class=\"ltx_caption ltx_centering\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure\"><span id=\"S1.F1.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Figure 1</span>: </span>Overview of the proposed model.</figcaption>\n242\t</figure>\n243\t<div id=\"S1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n244\t<p id=\"S1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Representing music as sequences of discrete units has been one of the central abstractions underlying computational musicology and symbolic music information retrieval. Its analytical value lies in the fact that transitions between musical events can be counted and modeled statistically. For example, corpus-based chord progression analysis <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib21\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">12</a>]</cite> can construct transition matrices between chord symbols and examine their distributional properties, which is possible because harmonic events are represented as discrete categories. A similar principle underlies probabilistic models of melody: once a melody is represented as a sequence of symbolic events, the probability or information content of each note can be estimated from its musical context. Temperley’s theory of uniform information density in music builds on this idea, explaining musical organization in terms of the probability and information density of musical events <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib23\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">21</a>]</cite>. The same logic also extends to performance data: MIDI represents performances as note events with onset time, duration, pitch, and velocity, allowing timing and dynamics to be analyzed as event-level attributes <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib22\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">24</a>]</cite>. In this sense, symbolic representations are not merely encodings of music, but interfaces that make musical structure available to computational analysis.</p>\n245\t</div>\n246\t<div id=\"S1.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n247\t<p id=\"S1.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">The utility of discrete representations, however, is fundamentally contingent on the availability of appropriate discrete units. Automatic music transcription (AMT) has made substantial progress in converting audio into note-level symbolic representations, yet existing systems are developed and evaluated primarily on Western music <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib11\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">2</a>]</cite>. This limitation is not merely a matter of musical style or timbral diversity; it reflects an assumption that musically meaningful events can be segmented into note-like units with relatively well-defined pitch and duration. Many musical traditions do not fully conform to this premise. In such traditions, expressive content is often realized as continuous pitch movement rather than as a succession of clearly bounded note events, making conventional transcription pipelines ill-suited as a basis for analysis.</p>\n248\t</div>\n249\t<div id=\"S1.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n250\t<p id=\"S1.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">One way to obtain discrete units in such traditions is to rely on human expertise.\n251\tSome studies use expert-labeled events directly: for instance, a recent study on Carnatic music used expert-labeled svara and svara-form annotations to show that the svara-form performed for a given svara is strongly influenced by neighboring svaras <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib5\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">16</a>]</cite>.\n252\tOthers define a vocabulary of intermediate units in advance and transcribe recordings into it: the IDTAP platform, for example, represents Hindustani melodic practice using manually specified pitch-contour trajectories as a middle-level representation between raw F0 curves and conventional notation <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib12\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">13</a>]</cite>.\n253\tBoth directions show that discrete representations of contour-centric music can support analysis, but they share a reliance on predefined categories and manual labor, which is costly to scale and ill-suited to traditions where the relevant ornamental vocabulary is not formally codified.\n254\tThis motivates a different question: can we learn a discrete vocabulary of contour units directly from data?</p>\n255\t</div>\n256\t<div id=\"S1.p4\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n257\t<p id=\"S1.p4.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">In this work, we investigate this question through VQ-VAE-based pitch contour tokenization.\n258\tVQ-VAE provides a natural framework for this purpose because it learns a finite set of latent codes while preserving enough information to reconstruct the input contour. We use this mechanism not as a generative objective in itself, but as a way to induce a compact vocabulary of local contour patterns that can be used for musical analysis.\n259\tWe apply this approach to <span id=\"S1.p4.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">sigimsae</span>, the ornamental system of Korean traditional music, where expressive musical units are often realized as continuous pitch movements rather than conventional note events.</p>\n260\t</div>\n261\t<div id=\"S1.p5\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n262\t<p id=\"S1.p5.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Our contribution is threefold: (i) a VQ-VAE-based pitch-contour tokenization model that learns a discrete vocabulary of local contour patterns without labels; (ii) a transformation-minimized reconstruction loss that makes token learning robust to small temporal and pitch-domain variations; and (iii) an evaluation via segmentation-shift consistency, <span id=\"S1.p5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">sigimsae</span> classification, and qualitative mode analysis in <span id=\"S1.p5.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">pansori</span>. Our code and a demo page with audio examples are publicly available.<span id=\"footnote1\" class=\"ltx_note ltx_role_footnote\"><sup class=\"ltx_note_mark\">1</sup><span class=\"ltx_note_outer\"><span class=\"ltx_note_content\"><sup class=\"ltx_note_mark\">1</sup>\n263\t            <span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_note\">1</span>\n264\t            \n265\t            \n266\t            \n267\t          Codebase: <a href=\"https://github.com/SeongUkJu/pitch-contour-tokenizer\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter\">https://github.com/SeongUkJu/pitch-contour-tokenizer</a>, Demo: <a href=\"https://seongukju.github.io/pitch-contour-tokenizer-page/\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter\">https://seongukju.github.io/pitch-contour-tokenizer-page/</a></span></span></span></p>\n268\t</div>\n269\t</section>\n270\t<section id=\"S2\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n271\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">2 </span>Related Work</h2>\n272\t\n273\t<section id=\"S2.SS1\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n274\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">2.1 </span>Pitch-Contour Abstractions for Ornamental Music</h3>\n275\t\n276\t<div id=\"S2.SS1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n277\t<p id=\"S2.SS1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">A notable precedent is found in Indian art music, where quantized pitch contours have served as an intermediate representation capturing ornamental detail beyond note-level transcription.\n278\tRanjani et al. <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib1\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">17</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib15\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">18</a>]</cite> mapped each pitch value onto a music-theoretically defined grid of note positions and showed that quantizing only at contour critical points preserves rāga and gamaka characteristics.\n279\tShikarpur et al. <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib8\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">20</a>]</cite> adopted finely quantized pitch contours (10-cent bins) as an intermediate representation for hierarchical generative modeling of Hindustani vocal music.\n280\tBoth discretize pitch <span id=\"S2.SS1.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">values</span> onto a predefined grid; we instead learn a vocabulary over local pitch <span id=\"S2.SS1.p1.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">patterns</span> (fixed-length contour segments) directly from unlabeled data.</p>\n281\t</div>\n282\t<div id=\"S2.SS1.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n283\t<p id=\"S2.SS1.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">A separate line of work mines recurring melodic patterns from raw contours via similarity search.\n284\tGulati et al. <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib3\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">6</a>]</cite> used DTW-based similarity over a 365-hour Carnatic corpus, and Nuttall et al. <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib13\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">14</a>]</cite> applied the matrix profile to find repeated motifs within a single recording.\n285\tThese methods identify a small set of variable-length, recurrence-based instances that highlight reuse, but they do not yield a reusable vocabulary that covers every frame.\n286\tOur tokenizer, by contrast, aims to produce a dense token sequence over the entire contour using a fixed codebook, which makes the resulting representation directly amenable to corpus-level statistical analysis.</p>\n287\t</div>\n288\t</section>\n289\t<section id=\"S2.SS2\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n290\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">2.2 </span>VQ-VAE and Discrete Representation Learning</h3>\n291\t\n292\t<div id=\"S2.SS2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n293\t<p id=\"S2.SS2.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">VQ-VAE <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib6\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">23</a>]</cite> learns discrete latent codes through\n294\treconstruction-based training. Given an input <math id=\"S2.SS2.p1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{x}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, an encoder\n295\t<math id=\"S2.SS2.p1.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"f_{\\theta}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_{\\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> produces a continuous embedding <math id=\"S2.SS2.p1.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{z}_{e}=f_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{x})\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{z}_{e}=f_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>,\n296\twhich is replaced by its nearest entry in a learned codebook\n297\t<math id=\"S2.SS2.p1.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathcal{E}=\\{\\mathbf{e}_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{K}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">ℰ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{E}=\\{\\mathbf{e}_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> via\n298\t<math id=\"S2.SS2.p1.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{z}_{q}=\\mathbf{e}_{k^{\\ast}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{z}_{q}=\\mathbf{e}_{k^{\\ast}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S2.SS2.p1.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"k^{\\ast}=\\arg\\min_{k}\\|\\mathbf{z}_{e}-\\mathbf{e}_{k}\\|_{2}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace=\"0.167em\">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k^{\\ast}=\\arg\\min_{k}\\|\\mathbf{z}_{e}-\\mathbf{e}_{k}\\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,\n299\tand a decoder <math id=\"S2.SS2.p1.m7\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"g_{\\phi}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{\\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> reconstructs <math id=\"S2.SS2.p1.m8\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}=g_{\\phi}(\\mathbf{z}_{q})\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}=g_{\\phi}(\\mathbf{z}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math>.\n300\tThe model is trained with</p>\n301\t<table id=\"S2.E1\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n302\t\n303\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n304\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n305\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S2.E1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{rec}}+\\|\\mathrm{sg}[\\mathbf{z}_{e}]-\\mathbf{z}_{q}\\|_{2}^{2}+\\beta\\,\\|\\mathbf{z}_{e}-\\mathrm{sg}[\\mathbf{z}_{q}]\\|_{2}^{2},\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">ℒ</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace=\"0.170em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{rec}}+\\|\\mathrm{sg}[\\mathbf{z}_{e}]-\\mathbf{z}_{q}\\|_{2}^{2}+\\beta\\,\\|\\mathbf{z}_{e}-\\mathrm{sg}[\\mathbf{z}_{q}]\\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n306\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n307\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>\n308\t</table>\n309\t<p id=\"S2.SS2.p1.2\" class=\"ltx_p\">where <math id=\"S2.SS2.p1.m9\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathrm{sg}[\\cdot]\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{sg}[\\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> is the stop-gradient operator and gradients flow\n310\tthrough the quantization step via the straight-through estimator.</p>\n311\t</div>\n312\t<div id=\"S2.SS2.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n313\t<p id=\"S2.SS2.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">In music and audio, vector quantization has been used primarily for generation and compression of audio waveforms or symbolic sequences <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib7\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">7</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib14\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">5</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib18\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">25</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib19\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">4</a>]</cite>, where codes are optimized for fidelity or bitrate rather than interpretability.</p>\n314\t</div>\n315\t<div id=\"S2.SS2.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n316\t<p id=\"S2.SS2.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Our use of VQ-VAE differs from both lines: the input is a one-dimensional\n317\tpitch contour <math id=\"S2.SS2.p3.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the learned codes are\n318\tintended as interpretable analytical units rather than for generation or\n319\tcompression.\n320\tIn speech, vector-quantized autoencoders applied to raw or spectral audio have been shown to discover units that align with phonetic content <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib17\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">3</a>]</cite>, supporting the use of VQ-VAE for unsupervised unit discovery from continuous signals.</p>\n321\t</div>\n322\t</section>\n323\t</section>\n324\t<section id=\"S3\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n325\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">3 </span>VQ-VAE on Pitch Contour</h2>\n326\t\n327\t<div id=\"S3.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n328\t<p id=\"S3.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">The overall architecture is based on VQ-VAE, using a stack of 1D convolutional layers, with two key design choices to enable interpretable, segmentation-robust (position-invariant) tokenization of pitch contours, as illustrated in <span id=\"S3.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\">Figure <a href=\"#S1.F1\" title=\"Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">1</span></a></span>.</p>\n329\t</div>\n330\t<section id=\"S3.SS1\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n331\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">3.1 </span>Receptive Field Separation</h3>\n332\t\n333\t<div id=\"S3.SS1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n334\t<p id=\"S3.SS1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">To ensure a 1:1 correspondence between each discrete token and a fixed-length pitch contour segment, the input is pre-segmented into non-overlapping windows of a fixed size before encoding.\n335\tThis prevents inter-token dependency during decoding and promotes interpretability of the learned codebook by constraining each token to represent a single localized contour segment.</p>\n336\t</div>\n337\t<div id=\"S3.SS1.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n338\t<p id=\"S3.SS1.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">To direct the codebook toward capturing ornamental contour patterns rather than absolute pitch levels, we subtract the median pitch value of each receptive field segment before encoding.\n339\tThis makes the objective insensitive to absolute pitch offset and encourages each entry to represent local movements such as oscillations and slides.\n340\tWhen needed for visualization of the full contour sequence, the decoded contour can be shifted back by adding the subtracted median.</p>\n341\t</div>\n342\t</section>\n343\t<section id=\"S3.SS2\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n344\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">3.2 </span>Transformation-Minimized Reconstruction Loss</h3>\n345\t\n346\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n347\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Because the input contour is divided into fixed-size non-overlapping segments before encoding, the assigned token can be sensitive to the arbitrary segmentation position.\n348\tFor example, if a vibrato pattern is shifted by only a few frames, the phase observed within a segment changes, although the underlying oscillatory pattern remains the same.\n349\tThis matters because the model is trained with a frame-level MSE reconstruction loss:\n350\teven when two contours correspond to the same local ornamental pattern, small temporal misalignments can lead to large reconstruction errors.\n351\tWithout an alignment-aware objective, the model may encode incidental frame-level differences as separate entries rather than a stable representation of the underlying pattern.</p>\n352\t</div>\n353\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n354\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We address this issue by minimizing the reconstruction error over four transformation factors: <span id=\"S3.SS2.p2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">(i) temporal offset</span>, which accounts for arbitrary segmentation position;\n355\t<span id=\"S3.SS2.p2.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">(ii) temporal scale</span>, which accounts for small duration differences;\n356\t<span id=\"S3.SS2.p2.1.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">(iii) pitch offset</span>, which accounts for residual vertical mismatch between the target and reconstruction; and\n357\t<span id=\"S3.SS2.p2.1.4\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">(iv) pitch-range scale</span>, which accounts for amplitude or range differences in otherwise similar contour shapes.\n358\tWe define a set of candidate transformations consisting of temporal alignment, pitch shifting, and pitch-range scaling.</p>\n359\t</div>\n360\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n361\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Let <math id=\"S3.SS2.p3.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be a median-normalized input segment and <math id=\"S3.SS2.p3.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L^{\\prime}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L^{\\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the decoder output, where <math id=\"S3.SS2.p3.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"L^{\\prime}&gt;L\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^{\\prime}&gt;L</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n362\t</div>\n363\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p4\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n364\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p4.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We define a transformed reconstruction candidate as</p>\n365\t</div>\n366\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p5\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n367\t<table id=\"S3.E2\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n368\t\n369\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n370\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n371\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S3.E2.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"T_{s,\\tau,a,b}(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}})=a\\cdot C_{\\tau}\\left(R_{s}(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}})\\right)+b,\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">⋅</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{s,\\tau,a,b}(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}})=a\\cdot C_{\\tau}\\left(R_{s}(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}})\\right)+b,</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n372\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n373\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>\n374\t</table>\n375\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p5.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">where <math id=\"S3.SS2.p5.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"R_{s}(\\cdot)\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{s}(\\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> resamples the decoded contour with temporal scale <math id=\"S3.SS2.p5.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"s\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S3.SS2.p5.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"C_{\\tau}(\\cdot)\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{\\tau}(\\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> extracts a length-<math id=\"S3.SS2.p5.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"L\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math> crop at temporal offset <math id=\"S3.SS2.p5.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\tau\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S3.SS2.p5.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"a\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math> scales the pitch range, and <math id=\"S3.SS2.p5.m7\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"b\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math> shifts the pitch level.\n376\tA length-<math id=\"S3.SS2.p5.m8\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"L\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math> crop is then extracted with temporal offset <math id=\"S3.SS2.p5.m9\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\tau\\in\\mathcal{T}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\in\\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>\n377\t</div>\n378\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p6\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n379\t<table id=\"S3.E3\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n380\t\n381\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n382\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n383\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S3.E3.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}^{(s,\\tau)}=C_{\\tau}\\left(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}^{(s)}\\right),\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}^{(s,\\tau)}=C_{\\tau}\\left(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}^{(s)}\\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n384\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n385\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>\n386\t</table>\n387\t</div>\n388\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p7\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n389\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p7.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">where <math id=\"S3.SS2.p7.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"C_{\\tau}(\\cdot)\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{\\tau}(\\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> returns a contiguous segment of length <math id=\"S3.SS2.p7.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"L\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math> starting at offset <math id=\"S3.SS2.p7.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\tau\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n390\t</div>\n391\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p8\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n392\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p8.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">The reconstruction loss is computed as the minimum MSE over all candidate transformations:</p>\n393\t</div>\n394\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p9\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n395\t<table id=\"S3.E4\" class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table\">\n396\t\n397\t<tbody><tr class=\"ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline\">\n398\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft\"></td>\n399\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center\"><math id=\"S3.E4.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{rec}}=\\min_{s\\in\\mathcal{S},\\ \\tau\\in\\mathcal{T},\\ a\\in\\mathcal{A},\\ b\\in\\mathcal{B}}\\frac{1}{L}\\left\\|\\mathbf{x}-\\left[a\\cdot C_{\\tau}\\left(R_{s}(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}})\\right)+b\\right]\\right\\|_{2}^{2}.\" display=\"block\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">ℒ</mi><mi>rec</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo rspace=\"0.577em\">,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">𝒯</mi></mrow><mo rspace=\"0.577em\">,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo rspace=\"0.577em\">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace=\"0.167em\">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">⋅</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{rec}}=\\min_{s\\in\\mathcal{S},\\ \\tau\\in\\mathcal{T},\\ a\\in\\mathcal{A},\\ b\\in\\mathcal{B}}\\frac{1}{L}\\left\\|\\mathbf{x}-\\left[a\\cdot C_{\\tau}\\left(R_{s}(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}})\\right)+b\\right]\\right\\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>\n400\t<td class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright\"></td>\n401\t<td rowspan=\"1\" class=\"ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right\">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>\n402\t</table>\n403\t</div>\n404\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p10\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n405\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p10.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Only the transformation attaining the minimum error is used for backpropagation.</p>\n406\t</div>\n407\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p11\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n408\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p11.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">This can be interpreted as fitting the model under the best alignment between the decoded contour and the target segment, rather than penalizing all frame-level discrepancies directly. This objective encourages the codebook to represent contour patterns that are stable under small temporal and pitch-domain variations.</p>\n409\t</div>\n410\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p12\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n411\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p12.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">For example, vibrato contours with similar periodic structure can be mapped to the same token even when they differ in phase or amplitude, because the loss evaluates reconstruction after the best temporal and pitch-domain alignment.</p>\n412\t</div>\n413\t</section>\n414\t</section>\n415\t<section id=\"S4\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n416\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">4 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>\n417\t\n418\t<section id=\"S4.SS1\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n419\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">4.1 </span>Dataset</h3>\n420\t\n421\t<div id=\"S4.SS1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n422\t<p id=\"S4.SS1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">In this study, we apply VQ-VAE to pitch contours of Korean traditional music. Although the proposed method is applicable to any pitch contour-based musical tradition, such as Indian art music or pop vocal singing, we focus on Korean traditional music.\n423\tThis is because continuous pitch movement is not merely an expressive embellishment but a central, defining element of the tradition. Unlike Western staff notation, in which music is conceived as a sequence of discrete note events, Korean traditional music, particularly its ornamental system <span id=\"S4.SS1.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">sigimsae</span>, is realized through fluid, continuous pitch trajectories in which the boundary between one “note” and the next is often ambiguous or undefined. This makes it an especially well-suited testbed for a model designed to discover meaningful structure from pitch contours without presupposing discrete note events.</p>\n424\t</div>\n425\t<div id=\"S4.SS1.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n426\t<p id=\"S4.SS1.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We selected an in-house <span id=\"S4.SS1.p2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">pansori</span> recording dataset consisting of approximately 280 hours of audio recordings.\n427\t<span id=\"S4.SS1.p2.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">Pansori</span> is a representative vocal genre of Korean traditional music, sung by a solo singer with barrel drum accompaniment.\n428\tIt is well known for its extensive use of melodic ornamentation, making it a particularly rich source of the ornamental patterns investigated in this study.\n429\tFor <span id=\"S4.SS1.p2.1.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">pansori</span> recordings, the vocal tracks are first isolated using HT-Demucs  <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib9\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">19</a>]</cite> to remove instrumental accompaniment.</p>\n430\t</div>\n431\t<section id=\"S4.SS1.SSS1\" class=\"ltx_subsubsection\">\n432\t<h4 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection\">4.1.1 </span>Pre-processing</h4>\n433\t\n434\t<div id=\"S4.SS1.SSS1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n435\t<p id=\"S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We extract the fundamental frequency (F0) with the pretrained CREPE <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib10\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">9</a>]</cite>, which estimates F0 values every 10 ms with a confidence score in <math id=\"S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"[0,1]\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.\n436\tThe extracted F0 values are converted to MIDI scale and normalized by median.\n437\tFrames below a predefined confidence threshold are filtered out.\n438\tWithin each receptive field segment, the gaps of up to two consecutive filtered frames are filled via linear interpolation.</p>\n439\t</div>\n440\t<div id=\"S4.SS1.SSS1.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n441\t<p id=\"S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Segments containing extensive unvoiced regions are discarded because our 1D CNN-based tokenizer assumes a dense continuous contour and cannot directly represent missing pitch values.\n442\tFilling such regions with a constant padding value would introduce artificial patterns into the input, which could be absorbed by the codebook and hinder the interpretability of the learned representations in subsequent analysis.\n443\tAddressing unvoiced regions more explicitly is left for future work.</p>\n444\t</div>\n445\t<div id=\"S4.SS1.SSS1.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n446\t<p id=\"S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Before filtering, the data are split 8:1:1 into train, validation, and test sets.\n447\tDuring training, a random start point is sampled from each filtered segment at every epoch, introducing variability in segmentation position, while the validation and test sets use fixed segments for consistent evaluation.</p>\n448\t</div>\n449\t</section>\n450\t</section>\n451\t<section id=\"S4.SS2\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n452\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">4.2 </span>Model Specification</h3>\n453\t\n454\t<div id=\"S4.SS2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n455\t<p id=\"S4.SS2.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">The encoder and decoder each consist of 6 stacked one-dimensional convolutional layers with a receptive field size of 128 frames and a codebook of 256 entries.\n456\tThe model is trained using the VQ-VAE objective <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib6\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">23</a>]</cite>, in which the standard reconstruction loss is replaced by the transformation-minimized reconstruction loss described in Section <a href=\"#S3.SS2\" title=\"3.2 Transformation-Minimized Reconstruction Loss ‣ 3 VQ-VAE on Pitch Contour ‣ Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">3.2</span></a>, combined with a vector quantization loss with commitment coefficient <math id=\"S4.SS2.p1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\beta=2.0\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>.\n457\tTraining is conducted for 50K updates using the AdamW optimizer with an initial learning rate of 0.001 and a batch size of 1024.\n458\tWe select the checkpoint with the lowest validation reconstruction loss.</p>\n459\t</div>\n460\t<div id=\"S4.SS2.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n461\t<p id=\"S4.SS2.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">The transformation parameters are set empirically.\n462\tThe decoder produces <math id=\"S4.SS2.p2.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"2L\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2L</annotation></semantics></math> frames, resampled by <math id=\"S4.SS2.p2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"R_{s}(\\cdot)\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{s}(\\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to lengths <math id=\"S4.SS2.p2.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"L_{s}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> approximately <math id=\"S4.SS2.p2.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathcal{S}=\\{1,\\,1.08,\\,1.2\\}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 1.08</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 1.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{S}=\\{1,\\,1.08,\\,1.2\\}</annotation></semantics></math> times <math id=\"S4.SS2.p2.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"L\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id=\"S4.SS2.p2.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"128\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">128</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S4.SS2.p2.m7\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"139\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>139</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">139</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id=\"S4.SS2.p2.m8\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"154\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mn>154</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">154</annotation></semantics></math> frames); all valid offsets <math id=\"S4.SS2.p2.m9\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathcal{T}=\\{0,1,\\dots,L_{s}-L\\}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{T}=\\{0,1,\\dots,L_{s}-L\\}</annotation></semantics></math> are considered as crops, where <math id=\"S4.SS2.p2.m10\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"L_{s}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is the resampled length.\n463\tPitch-range scaling and pitch shifting use <math id=\"S4.SS2.p2.m11\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathcal{A}=\\{0.85,1.0,1.15\\}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.85</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.15</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{A}=\\{0.85,1.0,1.15\\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id=\"S4.SS2.p2.m12\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathcal{B}=\\{-0.1,0.0,0.1\\}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi class=\"ltx_font_mathcaligraphic\">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\">−</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{B}=\\{-0.1,0.0,0.1\\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>\n464\t</div>\n465\t<div id=\"S4.SS2.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n466\t<p id=\"S4.SS2.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">For the comparison, we also trained an <span id=\"S4.SS2.p3.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Autoencoder Baseline</span>, adapting Nuttall et al. <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib4\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">15</a>]</cite>, who applied Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection (UMAP) <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib20\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">11</a>]</cite> and K-Means clustering to supervised GRU embeddings.\n467\tOur unsupervised variant trains an autoencoder on the <span id=\"S4.SS2.p3.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">pansori</span> dataset and tokenizes its latent embeddings via z-score normalization, UMAP, and K-Means.</p>\n468\t</div>\n469\t</section>\n470\t</section>\n471\t<section id=\"S5\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n472\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">5 </span>Segmentation Shift Consistency</h2>\n473\t\n474\t<figure id=\"S5.T1\" class=\"ltx_table\">\n475\t<table id=\"S5.T1.3\" class=\"ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle\">\n476\t<thead class=\"ltx_thead\">\n477\t<tr id=\"S5.T1.3.1\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n478\t<th id=\"S5.T1.3.1.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\" rowspan=\"2\"><span id=\"S5.T1.3.1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text\">Method</span></th>\n479\t<th id=\"S5.T1.3.1.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\" colspan=\"2\">KLD <math id=\"S5.T1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\downarrow\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mo stretchy=\"false\">↓</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>\n480\t<th id=\"S5.T1.3.1.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\" colspan=\"2\">Acc. <math id=\"S5.T1.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\uparrow\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mo stretchy=\"false\">↑</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>\n481\t<tr id=\"S5.T1.3.2\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n482\t<th id=\"S5.T1.3.2.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\">0–60</th>\n483\t<th id=\"S5.T1.3.2.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\">0–124</th>\n484\t<th id=\"S5.T1.3.2.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\">0–60</th>\n485\t<th id=\"S5.T1.3.2.4\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\">0–124</th></tr>\n486\t</thead>\n487\t<tbody class=\"ltx_tbody\">\n488\t<tr id=\"S5.T1.3.3\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n489\t<th id=\"S5.T1.3.3.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\">Autoencoder</th>\n490\t<td id=\"S5.T1.3.3.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\">1.747</td>\n491\t<td id=\"S5.T1.3.3.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\">2.085</td>\n492\t<td id=\"S5.T1.3.3.4\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\">0.100</td>\n493\t<td id=\"S5.T1.3.3.5\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\">0.115</td></tr>\n494\t<tr id=\"S5.T1.3.4\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n495\t<th id=\"S5.T1.3.4.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\">VQ-VAE</th>\n496\t<td id=\"S5.T1.3.4.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\">0.657</td>\n497\t<td id=\"S5.T1.3.4.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\">0.859</td>\n498\t<td id=\"S5.T1.3.4.4\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\">0.462</td>\n499\t<td id=\"S5.T1.3.4.5\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\" style=\"padding:0.6pt 5.5pt;\">0.488</td></tr>\n500\t<tr id=\"S5.T1.3.5\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "bb6552e5-3862-4aa0-94ec-2981ad455323", "uuid": "c1a5eafb-6c4a-497e-8405-83c3db789ae1", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T11:36:48.579Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608_10979_full.html", "content": "<!DOCTYPE html><html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta http-equiv=\"content-type\" content=\"text/html; charset=UTF-8\">\n<title>Pitch Contour Tokenization using VQ-VAE and Its Application on Korean Traditional Music Analysis</title>\n<!--Generated by LaTeXML oxide (version 0.7.5) http://dlmf.nist.gov/LaTeXML/.-->\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, i